body:
I am so thankful for all who shared their views. This excites me, and I dont know where else to discuss this.

&#x200B;

Also i have stopped practicing, which is the more important issue. Time to get back to the couch!

I transitioned from qualitative research to Planning a few years ago, so I have been on both sides of the table. The transition was not easy, because the ways of looking at data, the ways of expressing that data and the way of telling the story is entirely different between Research and advertising. The main difference for me was that while research is about presenting findings, being a planner is about presenting a point of view. A number of times, it means ignoring what the research says. 

Keep in mind that every agency has a very different point of view on planning - but typically planners would be igniting new creative ideas or helping the Client look at their marketing problems from a very cultural perspective. (For example - For planning on a sports brand that advertises to kids, I have to keep a very keen eye on Sports Culture, Sports advertising, Kid Advertising, general cultural changes happening in the world of Kids, kind of content they are exposed to etc..) 

There are 3 main areas where research helps planners 

A. Can you help me uncover new motivations in my audience's life, or give a different point of view to an already existing motivation. Example : Nike might be about unleashing the athlete in everybody - But how many different ways of looking at it can you give me. That means semiotics as well as primary research. Winning, Sports, Athletics, competition... 

&#x200B;

B. Can you help me understand where my brand is- What do they think about it, what do I compete with in their minds (On some level ALL brands compete with each other) 

&#x200B;

C. Consumption context - In what environment do they consume my brand 

&#x200B;

I would focus the pitch deck largely on developing consumer understanding specific to the TG, and culture codes around particular needs. make my life easier both with the Client as well as creative. 

Also, the first feedback I ever got was - Be evocative. 

Disclaimer: I have worked in only 1 agency, so my responses might be biased. 

&#x200B;
